// @run
// @log p
// @log n
// @log 5
// @hint redundant type annotation: `inner` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `outer` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// The two return annotations are DECLARED redundant, not removed. They are what the row's own
// witness is written with, and D940 is the standing reason not to act on this hint blind: a
// hint about INFERENCE says nothing about the LOWERING, and there is at least one filed case
// where following it turns a working program into a trapping one.
// Regression (silent-class-inventory D941).
//
// A generic-typed PARAMETER forwarded as an ARGUMENT to a second generic function refused at
// monomorphize: `unsupported argument type for `e` in a call to `inner``. This is the shape a
// factored matcher chain has — `toEqual → adjudicate → report` — and it blocked the natural
// factoring of `std:test` v2, which inlines its failure path instead.
//
// A RAW `T` FORWARD ALWAYS RAN (`f<T>(v: T) { g(v) }`); the refusal needed the generic STRUCT
// carrier as the argument. That asymmetry is the whole diagnosis. `monoArgTyName` has three
// channels and the first two ask the ARGUMENT NODE — and a clone shares its leaf expressions,
// so `e`'s recorded type is still the template's `Expectation<T>`, which no pin rung claims.
// The THIRD channel reads the instance's own parameter annotation, which HAS been substituted
// — measured, it reads `Expectation<i32>` at the refusal — but it sits behind a
// `monoAnnIsCloPin` gate written for a function-VALUED forwarder and never widened past it.
//
// So the answer was one read away, behind a gate that only closures could open. A generic
// application is now pinned by its own SPELLING, the same argument the variant rung makes:
// `e: Expectation<i32>` is an annotation a NON-generic function already lowers end to end, so
// the instance lands on the working path rather than beside it.
//
// PLACED AT THE THIRD CHANNEL, NOT AS A LAST RESORT — tried that first and it never fired,
// because several arms below it `return ""` outright and the cascade never reaches a bottom.
type Expectation<T> = { actual: T, negated: boolean }

function expect<T>(value: T): Expectation<T> {
  return { actual: value, negated: false }
}

function inner<T>(e: Expectation<T>): string {
  if e.negated { return "n" }
  "p"
}

function outer<T>(e: Expectation<T>): string {
  return inner(e)
}

// 1 — the row's witness: T inferred from a generic call's RETURN
print(outer(expect(5)))

// 2 — the negated arm, so the forward is carrying the FIELD and not just type-checking
function mkNeg<T>(value: T): Expectation<T> {
  return { actual: value, negated: true }
}
print(outer(mkNeg("hi")))

// 3 — the carrier's payload survives the hop and reads back at its own type
function actualOf<T>(e: Expectation<T>): T {
  e.actual
}
function fwd<T>(e: Expectation<T>): T {
  return actualOf(e)
}
print(fwd(expect(5)))
